### Description

Since #10554, browser tests are served by the project's Vite server. There, `vitest:environments-module-runner` configures every environment except `client` for the Node module runner. Custom environments with `consumer: 'client'` are consumed by the browser too, but they lose their `optimizeDeps` (and get `resolve.noExternal: true`, `keepProcessEnv: true`, `preTransformRequests: false`), so CommonJS dependencies reach the page untransformed.

Real case: [vitest-plugin-rsc](https://github.com/storybookjs/vitest-plugin-rsc) runs the browser side of React Server Components in a `react_client` environment through a module runner in the page. On 5.0.x it fails with `ReferenceError: module is not defined` at `react/jsx-runtime.js`; it worked in 5.0.0-beta.2.

Minimal reproduction:

```ts
export default defineConfig({
  environments: {
    page_runner: { consumer: 'client', optimizeDeps: { include: ['react'] } },
  },
  test: {
    browser: { enabled: true, provider: playwright(), instances: [{ browser: 'chromium' }] },
  },
})
// resolved `environments.page_runner.optimizeDeps` is `{ noDiscovery: true, include: [] }`
```

Fix: in browser mode, leave environments with `consumer: 'client'` alone in both hooks, like `client`. Vite only defaults `consumer` to `'client'` for `client`, so this affects only environments that opt in explicitly.

Written with Claude Code on behalf of @kasperpeulen, who has reviewed it.

